    "text": "Have you ever wondered how a refrigerator actually makes things cold? It\u2019s not magic, but it is a pretty cool trick involving a special liquid called a refrigerant. Think of this refrigerant as a tiny, busy worker that moves heat around.\n\nInside your fridge, there\u2019s a system of tubes. The refrigerant starts as a gas. A compressor, which is like a strong pump, squeezes this gas. When you squeeze a gas, it gets hot. This hot gas moves to the back of your fridge (where you might feel warm air coming out). There, it cools down and turns back into a liquid. This process releases the heat it picked up from inside the fridge into the room.\n\nNext, this cold liquid flows through a tiny valve. Suddenly, the pressure drops, and the liquid flashes back into a gas. This change requires energy, so it sucks heat from the air inside the fridge. That\u2019s why the inside gets cold! The refrigerant, now a cold gas, goes back to the compressor to start the cycle again.\n\nSo, the fridge doesn\u2019t actually \"make\" cold. Instead, it moves heat from the inside of the box to the outside of the box. It\u2019s like a heat pump. The food stays cold because the heat is constantly being pulled out of the air around it and pushed out the back.\n\nThis cycle happens over and over, keeping your milk, veggies, and leftovers at a safe temperature. It\u2019s a continuous loop of compression, cooling, expansion, and heat absorption. Next time you open your fridge, remember that a tiny, invisible worker is hard at work moving heat out so your snacks stay fresh and tasty!",
